From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:05:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dmaengine: apple-sio: keep the domain up until the
 sleep's commit stop

With the stop deferred to the commit of a firmware-driven suspend, the
SIO is still runtime-suspended when the noirq phase comes, so genpd
powers its domain down. The commit then reads its ASC CPU control and
polls its mailbox with nothing behind them: on T8132 both reads end in
a synchronous external abort and an SError, and every deep sleep on
the J604 stopped there, before the commit.

Put it on the wakeup path when it hands its stop to the commit, as the
display coprocessor does, so the domain (active-wakeup) stays up until
the commit has stopped it.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/dma/apple-sio.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/dma/apple-sio.c b/drivers/dma/apple-sio.c
index 5af7b41bcc..09d278ae4e 100644
--- a/drivers/dma/apple-sio.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/apple-sio.c
@@ -2174,6 +2174,13 @@ static __maybe_unused int sio_suspend_late(struct device *dev)
 
 	if (sio->commit_registered && apple_sleep_commit_stops_coprocs()) {
 		apple_sleep_coproc_set_ready(dev, true);
+		/*
+		 * The commit stops it, through its ASC and mailbox, after the noirq
+		 * phase: keep its power domain up until then, as genpd would power it
+		 * down with the device runtime-suspended. Read with the domain down,
+		 * both answer with an external abort.
+		 */
+		device_set_wakeup_path(dev);
 		sio->commit_used = true;
 		sio->asleep = true;
 		return 0;
-- 
